## What each result observed

### `kill-mid-turn`

| Variant | Outcome | Phase | Detail |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| baseline | **lost its position** | a tool call in flight in iteration 1 | Killed pid 79009 with SIGKILL while the runtime's tool call was at the gate (out tool.execution_start 2). plan before 'in-progress 0 16', after the kill 'in-progress 0 16'; files the half-turn left new: [none], files it left modified: [none]. A new process ran the same command: exit 0, plan 'complete 16 0', verifier 'pass', last line "plan reports complete after 1 iteration(s).". The baseline's position is a local variable, so nothing continued the killed turn: the second process re-read the plan and began at its own iteration 1. Whatever the half-turn had already written stays on disk and is not recorded anywhere — that is what 'lost its position' means here, not 'lost its files'. |
| durable | **refused to resume the dead turn** | a tool call in flight in iteration 1 | Killed pid 57113 with SIGKILL while the runtime's tool call was at the gate (out tool.execution_start 2). Store before the kill 'present 28 0 turn-started/0', after it 'present 28 0 turn-started/0'; plan before 'in-progress 0 16', after the kill 'in-progress 0 16'; files the half-turn left new: [none], files it left modified: [none]. A new process read that record and refused: the turn was issued and never finished, so re-running it could duplicate the writes it had already made. Its last line: "durable: run did not complete: failed iteration 1 was in flight: the turn was issued (plan digest 07426b11a9af6214) and its outcome was never recorded, so it ma". Exit 1, plan 'in-progress 0 16', verifier 'fail: FAIL  release-notes.md: '### Overview' expected', store after 'present 36 0 turn-started/0'. This is the variant's stated limit — nothing durable records what a turn that does not return did — reported as the outcome rather than smoothed into 'resumed'. |

### `restart-after-checkpoint`

| Variant | Outcome | Phase | Detail |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| baseline | **restarted from the plan** | stopped after a tool call returned, inside the pass | Before the stop: plan 'in-progress 0 16' (1 iteration line(s) printed), seam 'out tool.execution_complete 1'. Stopped pid 91582: wait reported 143, i.e. it died of signal 15 (SIGTERM is 15). A second process then ran with the same command and no resume capability: 1 iteration line(s) in the log in total (its own are appended), exit 0, plan 'complete 16 0', verifier 'pass', last line: "plan reports complete after 1 iteration(s).". The baseline keeps its position in a local variable, so the second process re-read the plan and began again at its own iteration 1; the iteration the first process committed is not known to it. |
| durable | **not exercised** | a boundary between two iterations, seen from outside the process | Not reachable, and checked rather than assumed. The only frame a process outside the run can stop at between iterations is the creation of the next iteration's conversation. At that frame the store already says 'turn-started/1' — iteration 2's turn was issued and has not returned — because the checkpoint that marks a turn started is committed before the conversation is created and the settle that follows is inside the same iteration. 1 iteration(s) had completed by then. So a process stopped here leaves the state kill-mid-turn records: a turn that was issued and never finished, which the next process refuses rather than repeats. A restart from a *settled* boundary exists only inside the process, and a fault injected from outside cannot land on it. The control below is the same run uninterrupted, which does finish. |

### `duplicate-delivery`

| Variant | Outcome | Phase | Detail |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| baseline | **doubled** | one iteration, one delivery re-delivered by the gate | One delivery, forwarded twice by the gate (1 re-delivery of session.sendAndWait). The second copy got a turn of its own: 5 tool call(s) crossed the gate after the re-delivery, out of 5 in the whole run. One iteration finished, exit 0, plan 'complete 16 0', verifier 'pass'. The side effect of a second delivery is therefore a second set of tool calls over the same files, not an error the loop reports. This is a measured count of frames, not an assumption about the variant: the same words would be chosen for either variant. |
| durable | **doubled** | one iteration, one delivery re-delivered by the gate | One delivery, forwarded twice by the gate (1 re-delivery of session.send). The second copy got a turn of its own: 10 tool call(s) crossed the gate after the re-delivery, out of 10 in the whole run. One iteration finished, exit 3, plan 'in-progress 2 14', verifier 'fail: FAIL  section-01.md: first line is not an H1: The queue keeps five jobs.'. The side effect of a second delivery is therefore a second set of tool calls over the same files, not an error the loop reports. This is a measured count of frames, not an assumption about the variant: the same words would be chosen for either variant. |

### `concurrent-clients`

| Variant | Outcome | Phase | Detail |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| baseline | **not corrupted** | loop 1 mid-turn; loop 2 started against the same target while loop 1 was blocked | Two loops over one folder overlapped — loop 1 (pid 9442) was alive and blocked at its gate on 'out tool.execution_start 2' when loop 2 started. Neither refused the other, both finished (exit 0 and 0), the plan is still a plan (intact), and the verifier passes. 1+1 iteration line(s) in total, plan 'complete 16 0'. Nothing serialised them and nothing broke: that is the finding, and it is not the same claim as 'serialised'. |
| durable | **refused the second client** | loop 1 mid-turn; loop 2 started against the same target while loop 1 was blocked | Two loops over one folder overlapped: loop 1 (pid 72018) was alive and blocked at its gate on 'out tool.execution_start 2' when loop 2 started. Loop 2 exited 4 and its own first line about the collision was: "refused:   The durable store file '/Users/core/git/matthewcorven/github-copilot-sdk-durable-worktrees/squad-151-work/.scratch/band-b-faults/durable/concurrent-clients/target/.durable/store.jsonl' is a". Loop 1 exited 3 after 2+0 iteration line(s) in total. Plan 'in-progress 4 12', shape intact, verifier 'fail: FAIL  section-03.md: first line is not an H1: Settings live in a file.'. The second loop did not read the plan, send a prompt or touch a file: the refusal comes from the layer's exclusive lock on the store file, and the variant reports it as a refusal rather than as a failed run. |

### `store-outage`

| Variant | Outcome | Phase | Detail |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| baseline | **not exercised** | the run's state on disk | The baseline keeps no state outside the job's own files, so there is no store to take away and nothing durable for an outage to fail safely over. Checked rather than assumed: the run finished (verifier 'pass'), and its target holds no file but PLAN.md, PROMPT.md, release-notes.md and docs/*.md — no store, no checkpoint, no session index. Removing one of those would fault the file the job is rewriting, which is a different fault and is not reported under this name. |
| durable | **continued past the outage** | a tool result in flight; store moved aside | The store was moved while the application held it open, and the run neither refused nor stopped: exit 0, last line "plan reports complete after 8 iteration(s).", plan 'complete 16 0', files new after the outage [none], verifier 'pass'. A new store stands at the path ('present 307 0 iteration-complete/8') because the open handle kept writing to the moved inode. The moved file is 'present 24 0 turn-started/0'. That is the honest description: an outage the run did not turn into a failure, reported rather than folded into 'failed safely'. |

